@noindent
A pointer to a number, such as @code{n4}, might consist of a type indicating
numeric data together with the actual representation of the number
4.@footnote{This decision on the representation of numbers determines whether
@code{eq?}, which tests equality of pointers, can be used to test for equality
of numbers.  If the pointer contains the number itself, then equal numbers will
have the same pointer.  But if the pointer contains the index of a location
where the number is stored, equal numbers will be guaranteed to have equal
pointers only if we are careful never to store the same number in more than one
location.}  To deal with numbers that are too large to be represented in the
fixed amount of space allocated for a single pointer, we could use a distinct
@newterm{bignum} data type, for which the pointer designates a list in which
the parts of the number are stored.@footnote{This is just like writing a number
as a sequence of digits, except that each ``digit'' is a number between 0 and
the largest number that can be stored in a single pointer.}

An atom compares by name: @code{equal_word} holds of two @code{Atom} words
with the same string, so two occurrences of one name are the same atom with
no interning table.  A @code{Bool} word is a test's answer and is never
stored in a cell by the book's code; @code{Vec} names a semispace's car or
cdr vector, and @code{Lab} holds a label as data.

@subsubheading Implementing the primitive list operations

Given the above representation scheme, we can replace each ``primitive'' list
operation of a register machine with one or more primitive vector operations.
We will use two registers, @code{the-cars} and @code{the-cdrs}, to identify the
memory vectors, and will assume that @code{vector-ref} and @code{vector-set!}
are available as primitive operations.  We also assume that numeric operations
on pointers (such as incrementing a pointer, using a pair pointer to index a
vector, or adding two numbers) use only the index portion of the typed pointer.
In this edition the two memory vectors are the two arrays of the working
semispace, and the selectors and mutators below are the functions over them
that the module installs as the machine's default operations.

The words of the memory are typed pointers, compared by @code{equal_word},
with @code{word_to_string} rendering a pair pointer as @code{p5}, a number as
@code{n4}, and the empty list as @code{e0}:

@example
type word =
  | Pair of int
  | Num of int
  | Atom of string
  | Empty
  | Broken_heart
  | Bool of bool
  | Vec of int * bool
  | Lab of string
@end example

@noindent
For example, the machine supports the operations @code{car} and @code{cdr},
implemented as one array read each through the index part of the pair
pointer:

@example
let car mem = function
  | Pair i -> read (cars mem mem.working) i
  | w -> not_pair "car" w
;;

let cdr mem = function
  | Pair i -> read (cdrs mem mem.working) i
  | w -> not_pair "cdr" w
;;
@end example

@noindent
Building the pair of 1 and 2 in a fresh memory and reading it back answers
the words @code{p0}, @code{n1}, and @code{n2}: the pointer the allocation
answered, then the car and the cdr.

@example
let car mem = function
  | Pair i -> read (cars mem mem.working) i
  | w -> not_pair "car" w
;;

let cdr mem = function
  | Pair i -> read (cdrs mem mem.working) i
  | w -> not_pair "cdr" w
;;
@end example

@noindent
The mutators @code{set-car!} and @code{set-cdr!} store through the index in
the same way:

@example
let set_car mem w v =
  match w with
  | Pair i -> store (cars mem mem.working) i v
  | _ -> not_pair "set-car!" w
;;

let set_cdr mem w v =
  match w with
  | Pair i -> store (cdrs mem mem.working) i v
  | _ -> not_pair "set-cdr!" w
;;
@end example

@noindent
@code{Cons} is performed by allocating an unused index and storing the
arguments to @code{cons} in @code{the-cars} and @code{the-cdrs} at that indexed
vector position.  We presume that there is a special register, @code{free},
that always holds a pair pointer containing the next available index, and that
we can increment the index part of that pointer to find the next free
location.@footnote{There are other ways of finding free storage.  For example,
we could link together all the unused pairs into a @newterm{free list}.  Our
free locations are consecutive (and hence can be accessed by incrementing a
pointer) because we are using a compacting garbage collector, as we will see in
@ref{5.3.2}.}  For example, the instruction

@example
(assign ⟨@var{reg₁}⟩
        (op cons)
        (reg ⟨@var{reg₂}⟩)
        (reg ⟨@var{reg₃}⟩))
@end example

@noindent
is implemented as the following sequence of vector operations:@footnote{This is
essentially the implementation of @code{cons} in terms of @code{set-car!} and
@code{set-cdr!}, as described in @ref{3.3.1}.  The operation
@code{get-new-pair} used in that implementation is realized here by the
@code{free} pointer.}

@example
let cons mem a d =
  if mem.free >= data_limit mem
  then failed exhausted
  else (
    let i = mem.free in
    let* () = store (cars mem mem.working) i a in
    let* () = store (cdrs mem mem.working) i d in
    mem.free <- i + 1;
    mem.trace
    <- Printf.sprintf
         "cons -> p%d = (%s, %s); free p%d -> p%d"
         i
         (word_to_string a)
         (word_to_string d)
         i
         mem.free
       :: mem.trace;
    Ok (Pair i))
;;
(* cons mem (Num 1) (Num 2) = Ok p0, and the trace records
   "cons -> p0 = (n1, n2); free p0 -> p1" *)
@end example

@noindent
Words compare by @code{equal_word}, and predicates such as @code{pair?},
@code{null?}, @code{atom?}, and @code{number?} need only check the type
field:

@example
let equal_word a b =
  match a, b with
  | Pair x, Pair y | Num x, Num y -> x = y
  | Atom x, Atom y | Lab x, Lab y -> String.equal x y
  | Empty, Empty | Broken_heart, Broken_heart -> true
  | Bool x, Bool y -> Bool.equal x y
  | Vec (k, c), Vec (l, d) -> k = l && Bool.equal c d
  | _ -> false
;;

let is_pair = function Pair _ -> true | _ -> false
let is_null = function Empty -> true | _ -> false
let is_atom = function Atom _ -> true | _ -> false
let is_number = function Num _ -> true | _ -> false
@end example

@subsubheading Implementing stacks

Although our register machines use stacks, we need do nothing special here,
since stacks can be modeled in terms of lists.  The stack can be a list of the
saved values, pointed to by a special register @code{the-stack}.  Thus, 
@code{(save ⟨@var{reg}⟩)} can be implemented as

@example
(* [push_stack m v] is (assign the-stack (op cons) (reg v)
   (reg the-stack)), with the monitored counters advanced *)
let push_stack m v =
  register_of m "the-stack" >>= fun stk ->
  machine_cons m v !stk >>= fun w ->
  stk := w;
  m.pushes := !(m.pushes) + 1;
  m.depth := !(m.depth) + 1;
  if !(m.depth) > !(m.max_depth) then m.max_depth := !(m.depth);
  Ok ()
;;
@end example

@noindent
Similarly, @code{(restore ⟨@var{reg}⟩)} can be implemented as

@example
(* [pop_stack m name] is (assign name (op car) (reg the-stack))
   and (assign the-stack (op cdr) (reg the-stack)) *)
let pop_stack m name =
  register_of m "the-stack" >>= fun stk ->
  match !stk with
  | Pair _ ->
    car m.mem !stk >>= fun top ->
    cdr m.mem !stk >>= fun rest ->
    stk := rest;
    m.depth := !(m.depth) - 1;
    Ok top
  | _ -> Error (Stack_underflow name)
;;
@end example

@noindent
and @code{(perform (op initialize-stack))} can be implemented as

@example
(* the book's (assign the-stack (const ())) -- the empty list
   of the memory is the word e0 *)
("initialize-stack", Action_op (fun _ ->
     register_of m "the-stack" >>= fun stk ->
     stk := Empty;
     m.depth := 0;
     Ok ()))
@end example

@noindent
Saving twice and restoring once in a fresh machine leaves the last saved cell
in the memory -- @code{the-stack = p0} -- and the counters answer
@code{total-pushes = 2 maximum-depth = 2}.  These operations can be further
expanded in terms of the vector operations
given above.  In conventional computer architectures, however, it is usually
advantageous to allocate the stack as a separate vector.  Then pushing and
popping the stack can be accomplished by incrementing or decrementing an index
into that vector.  In this edition the stack stays a list in the memory, and
that is what makes the collector of 5.3.2 reach it: the stack register is one
of the roots.

